Subject: cmd/link: emit a module with shared memory when the runtime has OS
 threads
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

A runtime with OS threads, which defines wasi_thread_start, runs each
thread as another instance of the module over one memory. For such a
runtime the linker imports the memory as env.memory, shared and with a
4 GiB maximum, rather than defining it; writes the data segments
passive, with a data count section; and adds a start function that
places them once, guarded by an atomic swap on a word the runtime
provides, so that the instances that are threads do not write them
again. wasi_thread_start is exported, with the type the WASIX runtime
calls it with, and kept through dead-code elimination, as is the guard
word, which nothing but the start function refers to. A runtime without
threads links as before.

A passive segment costs a memory.init sequence in the start function
as well as its header, so the split that omits runs of zeroes between
segments omits only runs of 32 bytes or more for passive segments,
against 8 for active ones. Go's data has many short zero runs, in the
pcln and type tables among others, and at 8 bytes a large program
exceeds the number of segments an engine accepts, with a start function
to match.

